description:
FILED s543m (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27), from the reviewing session's review of s543m (F1's "also in this family").  PRE-EXISTING: base 9a3ce86f the same; s543m's element-store flush (#5405) takes ONE level only.

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 | ./runpcl; scratch/s543m/p11/rows.pl prog 1):
    my $f = "/tmp/x.$$"; sub mk { open my $t, ">", $f or die; print $t "hdata"; $t } sub sz { my $s = -s $f; unlink $f; $s // "none" }
    my $self = { list => [ mk() ] }; $self->{list}[0] = undef; print "a ", sz(), "\n";
    my $o = { a => { fh => mk() } }; $o->{a}{fh} = undef; print "b ", sz(), "\n";
perl: a 5 / b 5.  PCL (base and tree): a 0 / b 0.

SAME PLACE AS F1? NO.  F1 widened the HOLDER question of `Parser2::_drop_store_form_p` (which store operator, which container atom); this shape fails earlier and twice: `Pl::Stmt::_one_level_elem` accepts one subscript after the symbol (or `->` + one), and the lowered store's container is an EXPRESSION, `(p-setf (p-aref (p-viv-array-container (p-gethash-deref $self "list")) 0) (p-undef))`, while `p-flush-dropped-store` requires a symbol container (read again by the store).  Direction: the shape accepts N subscripts; the macro binds a non-symbol container ONCE (the store vivifies it anyway; perl evaluates the constant RHS first, so the order is not observable) and peeks the slot.  Cost: one more `let` per such statement.
